WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.900
You may have heard that the United States
is moving toward cleaner energy.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:04.820
It’s true.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:08.300
Renewables such as solar and wind keep growing.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.000
Traditional fuels like coal make up less and
less of the energy we use every day.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.880
Many worry that this shift to new technologies
and clean energy might make jobs disappear.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.560
Instead, something else is happening, green
jobs are booming.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.640
But, let’s back up.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.080
What are these green jobs?

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.640
And where are they located?

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.660
The answers might surprise you.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:38.000
The two fastest-growing occupations in the
entire job market are solar panel installers

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.140
and wind turbine technicians.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.760
But, green jobs are much more than that.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:49.280
They also include clean energy construction
workers, designers, engineers, and high-tech factory workers.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.400
It includes people who make more energy-efficient
homes, offices, and everyday things

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.700
such as Energy Star appliances and electric
vehicles.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:04.360
Of the over 6 million people working in the
U.S. energy industry today, more than 3 million are already in these kinds of “green jobs.”

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:10.860
These jobs aren’t just in Democratic strongholds
like California and New York, which have embraced environmental regulations.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:18.100
Republican-leaning states such as Iowa, Oklahoma,
Kansas, and Texas actually lead the nation in wind energy.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:23.420
Arizona and North Carolina, both states that
went for President Trump in 2016, are among

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.200
the country’s top solar-energy producers.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:31.780
And the green jobs that come with this kind of production typically pay more than the national average.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:36.600
But as we power our lives with less fossil
fuel and more clean power, many people in

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.180
traditional energy industries worry they will
be left behind.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.000
Take the coal industry.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.020
Coal jobs have been disappearing for decades
as natural gas production increased and as

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.280
automation took hold in coal mines.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:54.080
From a high near one million workers, now less than two hundred thousand Americans make their living in coal.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.240
That's fewer than those working in solar
energy today.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.260
This change has devastated communities across
Appalachia and parts of the Midwest.

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:05.800
Environmental regulations are often blamed
for this downturn.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.440
But, in reality, gas just became cheaper.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.200
Solar and wind are becoming more profitable
too.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:17.540
And the switch to clean energy has the added advantage of helping the U.S. reduce its carbon emissions.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:22.040
Instead of propping up dying industries and
promoting jobs that aren’t likely to rebound,

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:26.440
state and local governments could be helping
workers make the switch to jobs in growing

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:31.420
fields such as healthcare, information security,
and clean energy.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:36.760
That means funding programs that make it easier
for workers to move from one part of the country to another.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.540
It means backing job retraining for the news skills
needed in the workplace.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.620
Washington remains sharply divided over this
green energy future.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:48.660
But new green technologies are already changing the energy landscape.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.440
And they are changing job markets right along
with it.

